---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import NumberToWordsConverter from '@/components/tools/everyday/NumberToWordsConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="number-to-words-converter">
  <NumberToWordsConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the converter</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        On <strong>Number → words</strong>, type a number and read the cardinal spelling, the ordinal
        form and the cheque-writing currency wording. Choose British "and", the lakh/crore system and a
        capitalisation style to suit the document.
      </li>
      <li>
        On <strong>Words → number</strong>, paste a number written in English words and get the digits.
        The tool reads hyphens, "and", "point" for decimals, and lakh/crore.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Writing an amount on a cheque</h2>
    <p>
      The words line on a cheque is the legal amount. When the words and the figures disagree, most
      banking codes, including the US Uniform Commercial Code, tell the bank to pay the words. So get it
      right:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Write the whole units in words, then the fractional part as <strong>xx/100</strong> or in
        words: "One thousand two hundred dollars and 50/100", or "one thousand two hundred dollars and
        fifty cents". Banks accept both.
      </li>
      <li>Start at the far left of the line, so nobody can add words in front.</li>
      <li>Draw a horizontal line through the space after the wording.</li>
      <li>Make sure the words match the numeric box exactly, including the cents.</li>
      <li>For a whole amount, write "and 00/100". Do not leave the cents blank.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Capitalisation is house style, not law. Sentence case is the most common. Title case is used on
      formal instruments and invoices.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules English uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Hyphens join the tens and units of two-digit numbers from 21 to 99: twenty-one, forty-seven,
        ninety-nine.
      </li>
      <li>
        "And" comes before the final part under a hundred in British, Irish, Australian and Indian
        English: "one hundred and five", "two thousand and forty-six". American English usually leaves
        it out.
      </li>
      <li>
        Written-out numbers need no commas, but legal drafting sometimes puts them between scale groups.
      </li>
      <li>
        Hundred, thousand and million stay singular after a number: three hundred, not three hundreds.
      </li>
      <li>
        Decimals are read digit by digit after "point": 2.75 is "two point seven five", not "two point
        seventy-five".
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Cardinal and ordinal numbers</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Number</th>
            <th scope="col">Cardinal</th>
            <th scope="col">Ordinal in words</th>
            <th scope="col">Ordinal in figures</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>one</td><td>first</td><td>1st</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>two</td><td>second</td><td>2nd</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>three</td><td>third</td><td>3rd</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>five</td><td>fifth</td><td>5th</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>eight</td><td>eighth</td><td>8th</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>nine</td><td>ninth</td><td>9th</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12</td><td>twelve</td><td>twelfth</td><td>12th</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20</td><td>twenty</td><td>twentieth</td><td>20th</td></tr>
          <tr><td>21</td><td>twenty-one</td><td>twenty-first</td><td>21st</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td>one hundred</td><td>one hundredth</td><td>100th</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td>one thousand</td><td>one thousandth</td><td>1,000th</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Only the last word changes. All words before it keep their cardinal form, so 1,203 is "one thousand
      two hundred and third", not "first thousand…".
    </p>

    <h2>Short scale, long scale and the Indian system</h2>
    <p>
      English-speaking countries now use the short scale, where each new name is a thousand times the
      last: thousand, million, billion (10⁹), trillion (10¹²). The older long scale, still used in much
      of continental Europe, makes a billion a million million (10¹²). This causes real errors in
      translated financial documents.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Indian system groups differently: three digits, then twos, which gives lakh (10⁵) and crore
      (10⁷).
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Digits</th>
            <th scope="col">Short scale</th>
            <th scope="col">Indian system</th>
            <th scope="col">Indian digit grouping</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td>one thousand</td><td>one thousand</td><td>1,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100,000</td><td>one hundred thousand</td><td>one lakh</td><td>1,00,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>one million</td><td>ten lakh</td><td>10,00,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000,000</td><td>ten million</td><td>one crore</td><td>1,00,00,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000,000,000</td><td>one billion</td><td>one arab / one hundred crore</td><td>1,00,00,00,000</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Precision on very large numbers</h2>
    <p>
      JavaScript's ordinary numbers lose exactness above about 9 quadrillion, so a converter built on them
      turns 9,007,199,254,740,993 into a different number. This number to words converter parses the
      integer part as a BigInt and never converts it to a float, so each digit you type is the digit it
      spells out, up to duodecillion (42 digits).
    </p>

    <h2>Where written-out numbers are required</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Cheques and money orders</li>
      <li>Contracts, deeds and promissory notes, where figures are often repeated in words</li>
      <li>Invoices and purchase orders in jurisdictions that require an amount in words</li>
      <li>Academic and journalistic style guides, which usually spell out numbers under ten or under a hundred</li>
      <li>Accessibility and screen-reader scripts, where a spelled-out figure removes ambiguity</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Amounts you type, including cheque values, stay in your browser.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
